  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure builds in the wake of the departing low resulting\nin dry weather and seasonable daytime temperatures through \nWednesday night. Winds will ease tonight and combined with \nclear skies, minimum temperatures overnight are likely to drop \ninto the low 50s (about 5-10 degrees below normal). This could \nlead to the development of patchy fog across the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nRain chances return Thursday, as the next shortwave trough \ndrops into the Great Lakes. This will drive a frontal boundary \ntowards our region, however, there are indications that the \nboundary may stall somewhere in the Ohio Valley area into the \nweekend. This is backed up by the upper-level pattern showing a\ntrough stalled just to the northeast. \n\nWith a stalled trough, the potential for rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms will continue through at least Saturday.\nCurrently, there is a low flooding and severe weather threat\npotential, however, this could change as we approach the\nweekend.\n\nFor the first half of next week, we are watching the potential \nfor a warming trend with daytime temperatures climbing into the\nupper 80s to near 90F. This would be the result of a strong and\nstationary upper-level high pressure system.",
